---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
// Faithful port of the live docs/privacy.md (URL parity: /privacy).
// Wording preserved; internal links remapped to the Astro routes.
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · URML"
  description="This site counts page views with a self-hosted, cookieless analytics tool that cannot identify you and stores no IP addresses. Beyond that, it collects only what you choose to type into its forms. All of it is verifiable in the source."
  active=""
>
  <section class="hi-section" style="padding:72px 56px">
    <div class="hi-prose">
      <div class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:12px">↳ Privacy</div>
      <h1 style="font-size:56px;margin-bottom:24px">Privacy</h1>
      <p>This site counts its page views with a self-hosted, cookieless analytics tool that cannot identify you and stores no IP addresses. It is described in full below. Beyond that count, the site collects only what you choose to type into its forms. All of it is verifiable by reading the source.</p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The public site sets no cookies. Not even the analytics sets a cookie. (An admin area at <code>/admin</code> is gated by login and sets a session cookie for the maintainer only; ordinary visitors never see it.)</li>
        <li>No third-party analytics. No Google Analytics, no advertising pixels, no analytics company, nothing that profiles you or follows you across sites. The one counter this site uses is self-hosted on our own infrastructure and described in the next section.</li>
        <li>No loads from other companies' origins, with two spelled-out exceptions: our own analytics subdomain (<code>analytics.urml.dev</code>, next section) and the embedded media in blog posts (its own section below). A Content-Security-Policy header blocks everything else at the browser level, so if a future change tried to add an origin it would break visibly instead of leaking quietly.</li>
        <li>No tracking pixels, no read receipts, no click tracking. Browsing a page sends us one thing: the anonymous page-view record described in the next section.</li>
        <li>No JavaScript beyond the small bundled scripts that power the page tabs and filters, plus the analytics script. The analytics script sends the page-view record and nothing else; the rest sends nothing anywhere.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>The site counts visits with a self-hosted instance of <a href="https://umami.is" rel="noopener noreferrer">Umami</a>, an open-source, cookieless analytics tool. Self-hosted means the data goes to our own instance at <code>analytics.urml.dev</code>, running on a server we rent and control. No analytics company receives anything; there is no third party in the loop beyond the server's hosting provider, which processes network-layer metadata the same way Netlify does for the site itself (see the hosting caveat below).</p>
      <p>For each page view it records the page, the referrer, your browser, operating system and device class, and an approximate location (country, region, city) derived from your IP address. The IP address is used for that lookup and to tell visits apart within a single day, then discarded. It is never written to the analytics database. There is no cookie, no identifier that persists across days, and no profile of you as a person. We use the result to see which pages people read and roughly where readers are, nothing more. The data is not shared with or sold to anyone.</p>
      <p>You can verify the scope yourself: the analytics script loads from <code>analytics.urml.dev</code> and posts one small request per page view, visible in your browser's Network tab. Blocking that domain, which most content blockers do out of the box, breaks nothing on the site. If you do not see that request at all, analytics is switched off; the script is only included in the build when the instance is configured.</p>

      <h2>The contact form</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/contact">contact page</a> has a form. It is one of a few places on this site that take input, and each only does so when you deliberately fill it in and submit.</p>
      <p>If you submit it, the form sends what you typed (your message, the email address you provide for a reply, and an optional name) to Netlify, which hosts this site. Netlify stores the submission in the project's Netlify dashboard and notifies the maintainer so you get a reply. That is the entire data flow. The form uses no third-party service, sets no cookies, and loads no tracker. Spam is filtered with a hidden honeypot field, not a third-party CAPTCHA.</p>
      <p>If you would rather not use the form, the same contact page links the GitHub issue tracker for anything that can be public.</p>

      <h2>The blog digest</h2>
      <p>The <a href="/blog">/blog</a> page has a "subscribe" form. It is the second place on this site that takes input, and like the contact form, it only does so when you deliberately fill it in and submit.</p>
      <p>If you submit it, we store your email address as a subscriber record so we can send you the digest. We use Resend (<a href="https://resend.com/privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer">Resend's privacy policy</a>) as the email processor: your address is passed to them so they can deliver the email. We do not use tracking pixels, click tracking, or read receipts. Resend's "track opens" and "track clicks" toggles are explicitly disabled in our send calls.</p>
      <p>We use double opt-in: you will receive a confirmation email, and your address is not active on the digest list until you click the link inside. Until you confirm, the only thing we have done with your email is store it pending confirmation and pass it to Resend for that one confirmation send.</p>
      <p>You can unsubscribe at any time by clicking the unsubscribe link in any email we send you. Unsubscribing removes your record from our store and you will receive no further emails. If you cannot find the link, ping <a href="/contact">contact</a>.</p>
      <p>Retention: until you unsubscribe or the project ends, whichever comes first. We do not share the list with anyone.</p>

      <h2>Embedded media in blog posts</h2>
      <p>Some blog posts include media from third-party sources:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hero images from referenced articles.</strong> When the maintainer publishes a post that references a news article, the publish step fetches the article's Open Graph image and embeds it at the top of the post. The image is <em>hotlinked</em> from the publisher's CDN. Your browser fetches it directly from them. The site sets <code>Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>, so the publisher cannot see which page on urml.dev embedded their image. The Content-Security-Policy restricts image sources to HTTPS only.</li>
        <li><strong>YouTube videos.</strong> When a post references a YouTube video, the publish step renders a clickable thumbnail with a play button overlay (the "lite-youtube" pattern). The thumbnail itself is loaded from <code>i.ytimg.com</code> (Google's image CDN) when the post is opened; no YouTube iframe loads automatically, and no YouTube cookies are set. Clicking the thumbnail opens the video on <code>www.youtube.com</code> in a new tab, and at that point YouTube's normal privacy policy applies. This pattern also works for videos whose uploaders disabled embedding entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Vimeo videos.</strong> Vimeo embeds load from <code>player.vimeo.com</code> as an iframe on page load. The site's Content-Security-Policy restricts iframe sources to a small allowlist (Vimeo and, for legacy posts, the two YouTube embed domains).</li>
        <li><strong>Instagram and TikTok videos.</strong> Stay as plain links. Their embeds require third-party JavaScript, which this site does not load.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you don't want to load embedded media, browser extensions that block third-party requests (uBlock Origin in strict mode, NoScript, etc.) will prevent the loads without breaking the rest of the page.</p>

      <h2>The hosting caveat</h2>
      <p>This site is hosted on Netlify. Like any web host or CDN, Netlify processes the basic technical metadata needed to serve a page: your IP address, the request time, and the file requested. That happens at the network layer for every site on the internet and is outside this project's control. For ordinary browsing, URML does not add to that, does not read it, and does not receive it. For a form submission, Netlify additionally receives and stores what you submitted, as described above. Netlify's own data handling is in <a href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Netlify's privacy policy</a>.</p>
      <p>We spell this out rather than claim a tidy "zero data" because the honest version is more useful than the flattering one.</p>

      <h2>Verify it yourself</h2>
      <p>The entire site source is public at <a href="https://github.com/URML-MARS/urml-website" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/URML-MARS/urml-website</a>. The network property is enforced by the Content-Security-Policy in <code>netlify.toml</code> and is checkable in any browser's developer tools: load any page, open the Network tab, confirm every request goes to this domain, to <code>analytics.urml.dev</code> (ours, described above), or to the embedded-media hosts described above, and nowhere else. The forms post to this same origin (Netlify intercepts them); they do not call out to anyone else.</p>
      <p>Trust is the most valuable thing an open standard has and the easiest to lose. Collecting only what you deliberately hand over, and saying so plainly, is how you keep it.</p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>If this ever changes, the change lands in the public repository first, with the reason, before it is live. There is no quiet version of this page.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
